meta {
  name: remove-image
  type: http
  seq: 9
}

delete {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/admin/v1/menu/items/{{admin_item_uid}}/image
  body: none
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{access_token}}
}

tests {
  test("responds 200", function () {
    expect(res.status).to.equal(200)
  })

  test("the dish comes back with no photo", function () {
    expect(res.body.data.image_url === undefined || res.body.data.image_url === "").to.equal(true)
  })
}

docs {
  # Remove a dish's photo

Idempotent: a dish that already has no photo answers `200`, not `409`. A remove button that fails
on a double click is a worse bug than a redundant write.

Clears **both** `image_key` and `image_url`, so removing an uploaded photo cannot silently reveal
an older pasted URL underneath it. The stored object is then deleted -- but only if we minted the
key. A URL a restaurant pasted from their own website is not ours to delete.

**Works on a deployment with no storage configured**, unlike the two upload routes. That is
precisely when a manager is looking at dishes whose photos no longer resolve and wants to tidy
the rows, so refusing here would strand them.
}
